Let $W$ be a real vector space of dimension $N$, and let $U_1,\ldots,U_m,V_1,\ldots,V_m$ be subspaces of $W$, where $m\geq1$. If $U_i\cap V_i=\{0\}$ for every $i$, then $$\label{eq:subspace-bound}
  \sum_{i,j=1}^{m}\dim(U_i\cap V_j)
  \leq \left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{4}\right\rfloor N.$$

Let $X_1,\ldots,X_m,Y_1,\ldots,Y_m$ be real $d\times d$ matrices. If $X_i-Y_i$ is invertible for every $i$, then $$\label{eq:rank-sum}
  \sum_{\substack{1\leq i,j\leq m\\i\ne j}}
       \mathop{\mathrm{rank}}(X_i-Y_j)\geq2d_m d,
  \qquad
  d_m=\left\lfloor\frac m2\right\rfloor
      \left\lfloor\frac{m-1}{2}\right\rfloor.$$ The sum is over ordered pairs of distinct indices.

For any pairwise distinct real numbers $x_1,\ldots,x_{2s+1}$, set $$\label{eq:triangular-pattern}
  Q_s=
  \{(p,q):1\leq q<p\leq s+1\}
  \mathbin{\cup}
  \{(p,q):s+1<p<q\leq2s+1\}.$$ The values at $(x_p,x_q)$ for $(p,q)\in Q_s$ determine uniquely a polynomial of degree at most $s-1$ in each variable. Equivalently, the $s^2$ vectors $$h_{pq}=(x_p^a x_q^b)_{0\leq a,b<s},\qquad (p,q)\in Q_s,$$ form a basis of $\mathbb R^{s^2}$, with the coordinates taken in any fixed order.

